From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Project Luma <engineering@projectluma.org>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 04:00:00 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] Session snapshots and restore

The windows a person had open survive log-out, restarts, shutdown and
crashes, and can continue on another device (ADR-055).

lumaSession.js takes a snapshot of every application window in stacking
order: app, key, monitor (with its vendor, product and serial), workspace,
frame and floating rectangles (absolute and as a share of the work area),
state, minimized, always on top, focus, and the state LumaUI apps give.
It is taken on lock, suspend (under a sleep delay inhibitor), lid close,
log-out and shutdown, and at most once a minute while windows change, into
$XDG_STATE_HOME/luma/session (0700, files 0600). After the log-out
snapshot nothing more is written, so the windows closing as the session
ends don't replace it.

At login, the last snapshot is restored once (org.projectluma.session
reopen-windows). Each app is started; each window is placed in its first
configure, before Mutter places or shows it, from its key, title or
order; apps that don't reopen their own windows are asked for more.
When every window has drawn, the stack is ordered, the workspace switched
and the focused window focused. Another monitor layout maps windows by
monitor identity, then role, then position, keeping their share of the
work area. A partial restore says what it couldn't do.

org.projectluma.Session1 on org.projectluma.Session: TakeRestore,
SetWindowKey and SaveState for apps (each caller only for its own app and
windows), Snapshot, GetSnapshot and Restore for unsandboxed system callers.
